---
# THE FIRST NetworkPolicy IN THIS CLUSTER. k3s runs kube-router's netpol controller (it is on
# unless --disable-network-policy is passed, and k3s_server_args does not pass it; the log line
# "Starting network policy controller" is there from cluster bringup), so this is enforced
# rather than decorative — but nothing had exercised it before, which is why the deploy verifies
# both directions instead of trusting that.
#
# WHAT IT IS FOR. n8n's task-runner broker binds 0.0.0.0:5679 because n8n has no per-interface
# bind option, and a client that can reach it AND holds N8N_RUNNERS_AUTH_TOKEN can register a
# runner — which is arbitrary code execution. Under Compose the exposure was `apps` siblings.
# Moving to one flat namespace would have WIDENED it to every workload in {{ k8s_namespace }},
# including karakeep's headless Chrome, which renders attacker-supplied pages. This policy makes
# the move a narrowing instead: the runners pod, and nothing else.
#
# The token is still a gate, not a replacement for one. This is the layer Compose could not
# express.
#
# Since 2026-09-17 the file fences 5678 as well (#1926): the same policy, two ports, two peer
# sets. netpol-probe-job.yaml.j2 proves both fences on every deploy.
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: n8n-broker
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: n8n
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
    # 5678: traefik, where the Authelia two_factor middleware, the CrowdSec bouncer and the
    # rate-limit run, and monitor-bridge, the one in-cluster API caller (check_n8n dials
    # `n8n:5678` with X-N8N-API-KEY — roles/k8s/monitor-bridge/templates/env-secret.yaml.j2).
    # Until 2026-09-17 this rule carried no `from:` and admitted every pod (#1926); under the
    # namespace-scope baseline that made n8n the ONLY web workload whose port any pod could
    # reach, and n8n is the one that executes workflow code and holds encrypted credentials.
    # Kubelet probes need no rule: they reach the pod IP directly and kube-router does not
    # subject that path to ingress enforcement (the headlamp and registry policies run on
    # probes under the same shape). No scrape job targets n8n, so prometheus is not admitted.
    - from:
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: traefik
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: monitor-bridge
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: {{ container_item.port }}
    # 5679 is the point of the file.
    - from:
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: n8n-runners
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: {{ n8n_k8s_broker_port }}
